[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
We tested end-to-end reachability from the Office subnet (192.168.3.0/24) to multiple Internet Lab hosts (128.168.0.0/24) and found the path currently healthy. From office_pc (192.168.3.2) to pc2/pc3/pc4/pc5 (128.168.0.2–.5), pings all succeeded with 0% loss and ~1–2.6 ms RTT. Additional checks from other Office hosts (enquiry, exam_cell, tpo) to various Internet Lab PCs also showed 0% loss and similar latencies. The topology-wide reachability matrix similarly shows successful Office-to-Internet-Lab connectivity. This indicates L2 segments, inter-switch links, and inter-router forwarding along the router1–router2 path are functioning.

Mechanism: If there had been a real outage, we would expect persistent packet loss or asymmetric reachability; instead we consistently observed complete, low-latency replies, suggesting no ACL, routing, or interface fault is present now.

Localization: No specific device or interface shows abnormal behavior during testing; the Office VLAN, the WAN links between routers, and the Internet Lab VLAN all pass traffic normally.

Root cause: No active fault was found; the earlier issue was likely transient or already resolved before investigation.

Corrective fix: No changes required at this time. Please retry your access—connectivity is working now. If the problem recurs, note the exact time and target host so we can correlate with router logs and ARP/OSPF status; we can then enable short-term monitoring on router1/router2 and clear/refresh ARP on affected hosts if needed.
=== END ===